To: taya@NetBSD.org
Cc: pkgsrc-users@NetBSD.org
Subject: [PATCH] inputmethod/anthy-elisp: accept emacs26 through emacs30

inputmethod/anthy-elisp only accepts emacs21, emacs20 and XEmacs:

  EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED= emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20 \
                           xemacs215 xemacs215nox xemacs214 xemacs214nox

so on a machine with a current Emacs it stops in can-be-built-here.mk:

  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs21 emacs21nox emacs20 xemacs215 ...
  ERROR: No valid Emacs version installed found

That line has not changed since the package was last restructured, while the
Makefile itself has been touched as recently as 2024, so it looks like nobody
has tried a newer Emacs rather than a known incompatibility.

This matters because anthy-elisp is the only package that installs anthy.el
(apart from the tamago ones).  A ~/.emacs that does

  (load-library "anthy")
  (setq default-input-method 'japanese-anthy)

therefore has no way to work on any Emacs newer than 21 through pkgsrc.

The diff below adds emacs26 through emacs30 to EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED.
PKGREVISION is left alone: the installed files do not change, only which
Emacs the package will build against.

Built and packaged on NetBSD 11.0/i386 with EMACS_TYPE=emacs26nox, against
pkgsrc trunk fa7ad771c96929bf742033e6ccfd8bb45b9cab49.  Byte compilation of
src-util/*.el completes and the package installs as anthy-elisp-9100hnb6.

Note that anthy.el still refers to last-command-char, which was removed in
Emacs 26; users work around it with

  (define-obsolete-variable-alias 'last-command-char 'last-command-event ...)

in their init file.  Byte compilation only warns about it, so the package
builds, but it would be worth patching properly if you would rather carry
the fix in pkgsrc.

Thanks,

--
